<h2>The background</h2>
<p>BHEL&rsquo;s Trichy unit tendered in 2014 for transporting boiler components from the Sone Nagar railway siding to the Nabinagar project site, and awarded the work to Sarr Freight. When payment time came, BHEL resisted on the footing that the contractor had subcontracted the work to an individual in breach of the general conditions of contract. The arbitrator rejected the plea in an October 2022 award, granting the contractor about Rs 13.73 lakh in transport charges plus its security deposit, earnest money and bank guarantee amounts with 8 per cent interest, and dismissing BHEL&rsquo;s counterclaims. The Commercial Court dismissed BHEL&rsquo;s Section 34 objections in May 2025, and BHEL appealed.</p>

<h2>Engagement of resources is not transfer of the contract</h2>
<p>The factual heart of the case was characterisation. BHEL pressed a letter, emails, a letter from the project company and an incompletely filed work order to show the contract had been passed on. The arbitrator read the same documents as showing a limited engagement of vehicles — hired resources deployed by the contractor, which remained responsible for the work. No subcontract was proved. The Division Bench declined to re-enter that terrain:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;the Court cannot reappreciate the evidence or substitute its own view for the plausible view taken by the arbitrator.&rdquo; And on the appellate tier, quoting the settled standard: &ldquo;no independent evaluation is permitted on the merits of the award.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>The double filter of Sections 34 and 37</h2>
<p>The judgment restates the architecture with economy. At Section 34, factual findings and the weighing of evidence belong to the arbitrator; an error of fact is not a public-policy violation. At Section 37, the court&rsquo;s task narrows further still — it checks whether the Section 34 court remained within its bounds, following the line of authority in <em>SEPCO Electric</em> and <em>AC Chokshi Share Broker</em>. A challenger must therefore locate a defect of jurisdiction, natural justice, or patent illegality on the face of the award; disagreement with the arbitrator&rsquo;s reading of correspondence is none of these.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For employers and PSUs.</strong> If the commercial intent is to bar third-party involvement of any kind, the contract must prohibit it in terms — hiring of vehicles, equipment or manpower included. Litigating the gap afterwards through Section 34 is, as this case shows, close to hopeless.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For contractors.</strong> Keep the responsibility trail — who invoiced, who supervised, who bore the risk. The finding that the contractor remained in charge of the work is what separated resource-hiring from subcontracting.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For challengers generally.</strong> Documents filed in part, as BHEL&rsquo;s work order was, hurt twice: they fail to prove the point before the arbitrator, and they foreclose the argument that the finding ignoring them was perverse.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Section 37 dockets in the Delhi High Court are thick with appeals that are, in substance, second attempts at the evidence. The courts&rsquo; consistent answer — plausibility review, not merits review — is the price of arbitration&rsquo;s promise of finality, and judgments like this one keep the standard visible. The subcontracting point adds a drafting lesson with wide application across transport, construction and supply contracts: the clause means what it says, and an arbitrator&rsquo;s reasonable reading of what it says is, for all practical purposes, the last word.</p>
